  const GEMS = [
    {"id":"gem-chlebicek","name":"Obložený chlebíček","tag":"Born in Prague","title":"The two-or-three-bite sandwich","pattern":"chlebíček","story":"In September 1916 Jan Paukert and his wife Štěpánka opened a delicatessen on what is now Národní třída. The story Czech press repeats: the painter Jan Skramlík, a friend and regular, found open sandwiches too big to handle and canapés too small, and asked for something of two or three bites. Paukert cut a slice of veka, laid on potato salad, ham, egg, salami and a gherkin, and the obložený chlebíček was born. Masaryk and the comedian Vlasta Burian were customers. The shop itself ended badly: food inspectors shut it in February 2015 over hygiene, and it never reopened. The chlebíček outlived its inventor's shop by a century.","body":"","where":"Lahůdky Zlatý kříž, Jungmannova 34 (reopened spring 2025; classic chlebíček 49 CZK at the reopening)"},
    {"id":"gem-prague-ham","name":"Pražská šunka","tag":"Born in Prague","title":"A ham with a disputed birthday","pattern":"prague ham","story":"Prague ham is brined, cooked and lightly beech-smoked pork leg, and since February 2018 an EU Traditional Speciality Guaranteed: anyone, anywhere, selling it under that name must follow the recipe. Who made it first is less settled. Expats.cz credits the butcher František Zvěřina in a small Celetná Street shop in 1857; Radio Prague International gives Zvěřina 1870. All sources agree it was Antonín Chmel, with his larger works in Vinohrady (Radio Prague places him on Zvonařka), who made it famous and exported it. Egon Erwin Kisch wrote that nobody without 'bacteria of the genus Chmel' could make a proper one.","body":"","where":"U Zlatého tygra, Husova 17: 100 g pražská dušená šunka with butter and gherkin, 90 CZK (menu seen Sept 2026)"},
    {"id":"gem-pilsner","name":"Pilsner (Plzeňský Prazdroj)","tag":"Born in Bohemia","title":"The world's first golden lager","pattern":"pilsner","story":"On 5 October 1842 a Bavarian brewer, Josef Groll, hired by the burghers of Plzeň for their new Měšťanský pivovar, brewed the first batch of a pale bottom-fermented beer: soft Plzeň water, pale malt from an indirectly heated kiln, Žatec (Saaz) hops. It was first poured on 11 November 1842 at Plzeň's St Martin's market. Nothing that clear and golden had been drunk before, and pale lager now makes up most of the beer brewed on earth. Prague had it within five months: on 8 April 1843 the coachman Martin Salzmann carried two buckets of it to Jakub Pinkas's pub.","body":"","where":"U Pinkasů, Jungmannovo náměstí 16, the first Prague pub to pour it (1843)"},
    {"id":"gem-sugar-cube","name":"Kostkový cukr","tag":"Born in Bohemia","title":"The cube came from a cut finger","pattern":"sugar cube","story":"Sugar used to come as a hard loaf you hacked at with a knife. In 1841 Juliana Rad cut her finger doing exactly that and asked her husband, Jakub Kryštof Rad, director of the sugar refinery at Dačice in Moravia, to find a better way; she proposed the cube. The Court Chamber in Vienna granted him a five-year privilege on 23 January 1843, and 'tea sugar' in cubes reached the market that autumn. Prussia, Saxony, Bavaria, Switzerland and England bought the rights. Dačice has a granite memorial (1983) and a plaque on the old refinery (2003).","body":"","where":"Dačice municipal museum, South Moravia (a day trip, not Prague); in Prague, every cup of coffee"},
    {"id":"gem-becherovka","name":"Becherovka","tag":"Born in Bohemia","title":"A spa pharmacist's bitters","pattern":"becherovka","story":"Karlovy Vary was a spa town full of guests with bad digestions. The pharmacist Josef Vitus Becher received a recipe from Dr Christian Frobrig around 1805 and spent two years balancing it; in 1807 he sold it as 'English Bitter'. It is roughly 20 herbs and spices, still secret; Hedda Becher was the last of the family to know it. The flat bottle, designed by Karel Laube in 1866, has not changed. Continuously made since 1807, it is the oldest Czech spirit brand, and nearly 8 million litres leave the Karlovy Vary plant each year.","body":"","where":"Any pivnice, chilled, as a digestif; the Jan Becher Museum is in Karlovy Vary"},
    {"id":"gem-kofola","name":"Kofola","tag":"Food gem","title":"The cola the Iron Curtain made","pattern":"kofola","story":"Czechoslovakia wanted its own answer to Coca-Cola. A team under Zdeněk Blažek at the Research Institute of Medicinal Plants in Prague spent two years on a syrup called Kofo: fourteen herbal and fruit ingredients plus caffeine. The first bottles were filled in Prague in March 1960, and mass production followed at the state pharmaceutical enterprise Galena in Opava. After 1989 Czechs chased Western colas and Kofola nearly died; the Greek-born entrepreneur Kostas Samaras bought a soda plant in 1993 and his family secured the trademark in 2002 for 215 million CZK. Less sweet than American cola, liquorice-and-herb at the back.","body":"","where":"On draught (točená) in most Czech pubs: ask for a točená Kofola"},
    {"id":"gem-trdelnik","name":"Trdelník","tag":"Food gem","title":"The 'traditional Czech' pastry that isn't","pattern":"trdelník","story":"Dough wound round a spit, rolled in sugar and nuts, roasted over coals. It is the Hungarian kürtőskalács of Transylvania's Székely Land, with a first written recipe in a 1784 cookbook associated with Countess Mária Mikes; it travelled into what is now Slovakia, where the town of Skalica registered Skalický trdelník as an EU protected geographical indication in 2007. In Prague it appeared on the tourist streets around the 2000s, sold with the word 'traditional' and filled with soft-serve. Czechs mostly walk past it.","body":"","where":"Skalica, Slovakia, if you must; not Prague"},
    {"id":"gem-svickova","name":"Svíčková na smetaně","tag":"Food gem","title":"Two hundred years of cream sauce","pattern":"svíčková","story":"Svíčka means candle; svíčková is the beef tenderloin, named for its long tapering shape. The dish that took the cut's name appears in Magdalena Dobromila Rettigová's Domácí kuchařka, published in Hradec Králové in 1826: 1,150 recipes, written in Czech at a time when the educated classes cooked and wrote in German, and reprinted in dozens of editions. Today the word means the sauce as much as the meat: root vegetables braised with the beef, puréed with cream, then served with bread dumplings, a spoon of cranberries and a swirl of whipped cream. Sweet, sour and salty on one plate.","body":"","where":"Lokál Dlouhááá, Dlouhá 33: 325 CZK (menu seen 27 Sept 2026)"},
    {"id":"gem-hermelin","name":"Hermelín","tag":"Food gem","title":"A camembert named after a king's cloak","pattern":"hermelín","story":"French camembert reached Czech tables around the First World War; Czech dairies copied it, and the Pribina cooperative in Hesov, founded 1923, made a Přibyslavský camembert. The word camembert was protected, so the engineer Josef Hojdar, later head of Madeta, coined Hermelín: ermine, because the fine white rind looks like the royal ermine cloak. The name was registered on 9 February 1946. The pub way with it is nakládaný hermelín: halved, spread with paprika and garlic, sunk in oil with onion and chilli for days, and eaten with dark bread and a beer.","body":"","where":"Lokál Dlouhááá, Dlouhá 33 (nakládaný hermelín on the permanent menu, Sept 2026)"}
  ];
